WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.430
Our work is the first to look at how
humans impact ecosystems in a really

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.139
fine grained way. I'm Stefani Crabtree;
I'm an archaeologist and archaeoecologist.

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:15.590
So The Archaeoecology Project
is a project that brings together

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:20.279
archaeologists, ecologists, and
anthropologists, and computer modelers to

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:25.590
try to understand the ways in which
humans impact ecosystems and the ways

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.840
ecosystems impact humans. My name's
Jennifer Dunne; I'm an ecologist and a

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:36.719
complex systems scientist, and I study
ecological networks. An ecological

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:43.950
network is a representation of an
ecosystem, and one type of ecological

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:50.070
network is a food web. Most food webs
that have been compiled and studied have

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:55.410
been put together without including
humans. One of the things that we've

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.480
started to do is to use an ecological
network framework to pull humans

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.030
explicitly into ecological analyses. And
what's useful about networks is that

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:10.920
sometimes it's difficult to understand
what kind of effects it would have to

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.900
remove this plant – let's say it went
extinct – would that have ripple effects

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.680
throughout the whole network as the
other animals scramble to eat something

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.880
else? Would that create pressures that we couldn't predict unless we had this

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.189
networked approach? The other interesting thing is people don't just eat. Like a

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:34.770
lot of animals do, we build shelters. We
make clothing. We use things that we take

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.820
from the environment in important
rituals. And so in in The Archaeoecology

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.479
Project we're not just looking at food
consumption. We're looking at these

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.979
myriad ways in which we impact
environments. What we're driving at is

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:53.340
really trying to use food webs and
ecological networks to approach issues

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.409
of sustainability, of socio ecological
networks, in a new kind of way. Within our

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:02.219
team we've published two of the first
highly resolved food webs that include

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:05.340
humans. Jennifer Dunne led a team that published

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.770
a marine food web that included humans,
and then I led a team that published the

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.620
first highly resolved terrestrial food
web – that's for the

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.930
ancestral pueblo case study. We approached these in slightly different ways but in

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.269
the end what we are getting is this
ability to cross-compare the ways that

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:29.610
people impact ecosystems in different
ways worldwide. We're looking at multiple

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.890
sites where there is incredibly good
preservation, where we can really

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:40.140
disentangle the human effect from the
environmental effect, and where we can

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.549
look at a really deep diachronic
perspective. These six cases where we're

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:48.329
working are the ancestral Pueblo
Southwest; we're working in the Norse

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:53.069
Atlanti;c we're working in the Pacific
Northwest, where the indigenous peoples

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:57.930
there developed a deep time
coevolution with their ecosystem; we're

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.250
working in French Polynesia, which is
kind of similar to that Pacific

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.269
Northwest –&nbsp;a very different environment
but that very deep coevolution of

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:12.930
terrestrial and marine environments. In
Europe people were foragers and then

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.569
they move toward farming so we're
looking at some of those earliest

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:19.139
indications of that and then we're also
working in the western desert of

00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:24.209
Australia. We can look at these six cases
these six incredibly detailed cases and

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:29.190
look at the ways that people come into
their environments, that they experiment

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.760
with the environment and with the
ecosystem, that they develop practices

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.129
that are sustainable or sometimes non-sustainable, and we can take that and we

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:42.090
can learn from it. These systems that we're studying are

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:47.220
very different in some ways, and as we
start to understand the patterns and how

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.810
humans interact with other species and
use other species, we can understand

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.079
those patterns – whether they're similar
different across systems – are there

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:58.980
generalities or are there specificities?
And we can understand that in relation

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.980
to the constraints that climate presents,
or the opportunities that different

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.340
kinds of ecological habitats present, or
how cultural norms for example, or social

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.660
hierarchy affect how humans interact
with other species.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.320
There's also aspects of the work that
we're doing that will allow us to look

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.920
at simple versus complex interactions and

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.250
that gives us a way to quantify
technology use innovation and a variety

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.030
of questions around that so it provides
a new lens not only on sustainability

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:38.100
but also on interesting issues in
innovation and technology. Once we

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:42.480
understand people's roles better we can
start to ask more questions about the

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.530
sustainability of systems that include
humans in terms of how they compare to

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.250
other predators and how they interact
with all the species they feed on and

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:54.350
all the species that those feed on as
well.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.490
 

